Here’s a minimal, robust `sanitizeAvatarUrl` function that escapes double quotes and other common special characters to ensure the `src` attribute in the `<img>` tag remains valid HTML.

This solution focuses strictly on sanitizing the `user.avatarUrl` string to prevent parsing errors and the subsequent crash.

```javascript
const sanitizeAvatarUrl = (url = '') => {
  // Escape double quotes to prevent breaking the src attribute
  // Also escapes single quotes and other problematic characters like angle brackets if they exist
  return url.replace(/["'<]/g, '\\$&');
};

export default sanitizeAvatarUrl;
```

